latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the input grid:\n   a. Find the gray (5) dividing line.\n   b. Identify all vertical lines (consecutive non-zero, non-gray colors in columns) on both sides of the dividing line.\n   c. Note the positions and colors of these vertical lines relative to their respective side (left or right of the dividing line).\n\n2. Create the output grid framework:\n   a. Initialize a new ColoredGrid with 7 columns and the same number of rows as the input.\n   b. Fill it with zeros (black) initially.\n\n3. Map vertical lines from input to output:\n   a. For each identified vertical line in the input:\n      - Calculate its relative position within its side (left or right of the dividing line).\n      - Scale this position to fit within the 7-column output grid.\n      - Place the vertical line in the output grid at this scaled position.\n\n4. Process horizontal lines and individual cells:\n   a. For each row in the input grid:\n      - If it's a full horizontal line (same non-zero color across the row), fill the entire output row with that color.\n      - Otherwise, for each cell in the output row:\n        * Find the nearest non-zero, non-gray color in the corresponding section of the input row.\n        * Assign this color to the output cell.\n\n5. Ensure vertical line continuity:\n   a. For each column in the output grid that contains a vertical line:\n      - Identify the color of the vertical line.\n      - Fill any gaps (zero values) in this column with the identified color.\n\n6. Handle special cases:\n   a. If no vertical lines are found in the input, place a single vertical line in the middle (column 3) of the output using the most common non-zero, non-gray color from the input.\n\n7. Final validation:\n   a. Ensure the output grid meets all the requirements (7 columns, same height as input, valid colors).\n\nThis plan addresses the key observations from our reflection:\n- It considers vertical lines from both sides of the input.\n- It scales the positions of vertical lines rather than using fixed positions.\n- It preserves horizontal lines and handles individual cell colors based on proximity.\n- It's flexible enough to handle varying numbers and positions of vertical lines.\n\nThis approach should work for all the provided examples and generalize well to other potential inputs within the constraints of the problem."
